FAIL SUMMARY (what is missing)
F1 [NEW - not in CD-j] The Codex container can reach the HOST, incl. live budget2 :8080 (SPEC D3).
   The checker sits only on the internal network, but the network's own bridge gateway address
   is the host. Probe stub, harness run from TREE (run-probe.sh, probe2):
     container ip 172.26.0.3, gateway 172.26.0.1
     curl -s --noproxy '*' http://172.26.0.1:<port>/  -> "hostprobe"  (verifier's listener bound
       0.0.0.0 on the host), curl rc=0
     TCP connect only (no request bytes) to 172.26.0.1:8080 -> "TCP CONNECTED" (budget2 listens *:8080)
     172.17.0.1 / LAN / tailnet addresses: "Network is unreachable"; external DNS: fails.
   So Codex, with its sandbox bypassed, can curl http://<gateway>:8080/ and read live household
   data, which then leaves in the conversation. This contradicts SPEC §1 D3 ("no host
   filesystem, no budget2 :8080 (it binds *:8080), no LAN"), the CD1 task-table criterion
   ("172.17.0.1:8080 ... unreachable" = budget2 on the docker host) and what step 4's "ONLY on an
   internal network" is for. Missing: no host address may be reachable from the checker
   container, the isolated network's gateway included. The oracle check "P a host service on the
   docker host is unreachable" passes for the wrong reason: it only probes 172.17.0.1 (docker0),
   which is off-link. The oracle needs a probe of the checker subnet's own gateway (lead's item).
F2 [= CD-j F1a, reproduced independently] Step 7 "every occurrence ... in out/ is replaced ...
   on every path" does not hold. probe5 "ro": the stub writes the fake secret to /out/ro.txt
   (chmod 444) and /out/locked/in.txt (dir chmod 000). The harness wrote a PASS verdict. Afterwards
   grep -rlF <secret> finds: out/locked/in.txt and out/ro.txt (redact_path gives up on a failed
   O_RDWR open; os.walk cannot enter the 000 dir).
F3 [= CD-j F1b, reproduced independently] Step 7 "any secret appearing in last.json -> skip
   secret-leak" can be defeated. probe5 "trunc": the stub runs ": > /codex-home/auth.json"
   (reports "truncated") and puts the token in last.json. Result: VERDICT: PASS is written with the
   full token in it; the token is also left in out/last.json; the HOST auth file is now 0 bytes.
   The secret set is read from $CODEX_AUTH after the run, and the rw mount lets the container
   empty it.

